      "text": "To find the number of four-digit numbers \\(N = \\underline{a}\\,\\underline{b}\\,\\underline{c}\\,\\underline{d}\\) that satisfy all the given conditions, we will proceed step by step.\n\n1. **Condition 1: \\(4000 \\le N < 6000\\)**\n\nThis condition ensures that \\(N\\) is a four-digit number between 4000 and 5999. \n\n2. **Condition 2: \\(N\\) is a multiple of 5**\nFor a number to be a multiple of 5, its unit digit \\(d\\) must be either 0 or 5.\n\n3. **Condition 3: \\(3 \\le b < c \\le 6\\)**\n\nThis condition specifies that \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) must be integers between 3 and 6, excluding 3 and 6 (since \\(b < c\\)):\n\n- \\(b\\) can be any of \\{4, 5, 6\\}\n- \\(c\\) can be any of \\{4, 5, 6\\} but must be greater than \\(b\\). \n\nWe will consider each possible value for \\(b\\) and count the valid combinations for \\(c\\):\n\n- If \\(b = 4\\), \\(c\\) can be \\{4, 5, 6\\} (total 3 choices).\n- If \\(b = 5\\), \\(c\\) can be \\{5, 6\\} (total 2 choices).\n- If \\(b = 6\\), \\(c\\) can be \\{6\\} (only 1 choice).\n\nTherefore, the number of valid pairs \\((b, c)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n3 + 2 + 1 = 6\n\\]\n\n4. **Combining All Conditions**\n\n- For each choice of \\(b\\) and \\(c\\), \\(d\\) can be any of \\{0, 5\\} (2 choices).\n\nCombining all valid combinations, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total count} = 6 \\times 2 = 12\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{12}",
